we do have in the studio with us that  the Chief Inspector of Prisons who took  a look at manston Charlie Taylor Charlie  great to have you this morning  um let's just start with the with the  the basic facts here what did you  discover uh through your visits to the  manston center is it in your view and I  put this question to Robert General is  it in your review fit for purpose well  we visited originally in the summer and  though we had a number of criticisms  that we made of of the center actually  it was generally functioning reasonably  well as was Western jet foil at Dover  where migrants arrive off the boats I  then returned in September to have  another look and already we were  beginning to see some of those risks  that we flagged up in the summer  manifest themselves so people were  starting to spend longer on site people  were unable to make contact with family  people were staying in accommodation  that wasn't good enough what we're now  hearing is is that things have become  much worse that people are spending much  longer periods of time at the center and  uh and and that I've announced that we  will go back and reinspect in the near  future when you say that the  accommodation is unacceptable just just  to explain exactly what you mean by this  I mean ultimately this is a site at  which people are supposed to be spending  no longer than 24 48 hours that's right  it's a short-term holding facility and  that means people should come in they  should be quickly processed and they  should move on to suitable accommodation  what's happening at Manson when I  visited was people were sleeping on the  floors on on their rubber mats down on  the floors and then very thin blankets  or mattresses lots and lots of people in  a room  um all squished in together very  uncomfortable the the the room for  families has lots and lots of different  families all sharing the same room uh  very young children older children uh  and some and for a few hours that would  be acceptable but where people are  spending long periods of time there it  just isn't what about the health  concerns that there are on site we've  had reports of cases of Scabies of  diphtheria of MRSA I mean all of that is  it must be troubling to you as well well  it's inevitable if if you put people  together in very close proximity for  long periods of time particularly people  who've come from parts of the world  where perhaps they haven't had the  opportunity to have vaccines uh  unfortunately the risk is that things  like scabies or diphtheria will appear  personally in your position and I know  this because my grandfather actually  worked in the inspector for Education  you have to choose your language very  very carefully when you tell the home  office that they need to get a grip on  this situation that sounds to me like  the strongest criticism that someone in  your position can possibly make  we'll be back we'll be back at the  center we'll be looking to see an  improvement in in treatment and  conditions of the detainees who are  there and yes they need to get a grip is  this situation solvable is it fixable  and if so home I think it is fixable  um providing suitable accommodation for  people uh making sure that the processes  that happen in manston are efficient  that they're streamlined that there are  enough staff enabled in order to do that  now there are many complications it's a  difficult site to run but we've seen it  operating pretty well when we were there  in the summer so this is doable but for  whatever reason things seem to have  unraveled more recently just just in  terms of of the day job and you go  you're in and out of Prisons up and down  this country comparing manston to to you  know your average prison the idea that  someone can't use the toilet without  having the door open the idea that  people are not being having access to to  Fresh Air I mean it strikes me that  those are things that would be  unacceptable and rarely to be found in  the prison system I would be horrified  if I went into a prison where prisoners  didn't have mattresses where prisoners  didn't have flushing lavatories where  prisoners didn't have uh telephones that  they could stay in touch with family and  friends those are three things that we  see at Manson similarly if they couldn't  get out into the fresh air and of course  one thing that you don't find in well in  in mainstream prisons you find in young  offenders institutions young people just  a just a word from you on the way in  which they're being treated we had  anecdotally you know one child had been  detained there for for longer than 40  well for 48 hours other children are as  you mentioned sleeping on the mat I mean  that in and of itself should be enough  to prompt a government into action it's  extremely concerning that children are  being asked to sleep on the floor in a  combination that's that's wholly wholly  unsuitable and bear in mind that some of  these people have had incredibly long  Journeys some of them are potentially  victims of torture victims of all sorts  of abuse and also potentially victims of  trafficking as well and the idea that we  then expect them to sleep on the floor  for long periods of time just is not  acceptable so what happens next from  your position what is the time scale  that the home office has to get this  sorted out and if it doesn't and you  mentioned you're going back into to see  what happens and if it doesn't improve  what is the following step well we we  will we will return to the site we we  won't announce when that will be we'll  return to the site we will expect to see  substantial changes in in what's  happening there we'll expect to see  migrants moving on more quickly to  suitable accommodation and that many of  the things that we've flagged up in this  report in terms of uh seeking out people  with vulnerabilities making sure the  victims of torture are identified making  sure translation services are in pl